  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nA delightful afternoon across central and southeast IL with\nample sunshine (some tufts of high clouds east of I-55 and north\nof I-70) and light winds and comfortable humidity levels. \nTemperatures were in the mid to upper 70s, with Olney and Mt \nCarmel airport at 80F. Canadian high pressure over Ontario and \nQuebec provinces continued to ridge across the western Great \nLakes and into IL and toward the Ozarks providing the nice wx.\n \nHigh pressure to start shifting east of IL during overnight\ntonight and will continue fair skies with light winds again\ntonight. Latest CAM runs show the fog forming further ne\nlater tonight and early Tue morning but did add patchy fog to\nVermilion county. A few CAM members like NAM3 km and GFSLAMP\nshow some fog forming further sw over east central IL late \ntonight so will need to watch for this. But RAP and HRRR keep \nthe fog mostly ne of central IL similar to Saturday night/Sunday\nmorning. Lows overnight in the upper 40s and lower 50s. \n\nSurface high pressure continues eastward journey into mid\nAtlantic States and New England by sunset Tue while upper level\nridge builds over IL Tue and Wed. The 500 mb height of 588\ndecameters approaches southeast IL by Wed evening and will see\nsummerlike temperatures by Wed over se half of CWA. Highs Tue\nmostly in the lower 80s climb into the 80s on Wed (ranging from\nupper 70s by Galesburg to 90F over Lawrence county). \n\nDry conditions should continue over CWA through Tue night. A\ncutoff upper level low over southern CA to move over NM on Wed\nand to pick up remnants of hurricane Polo (lifting ne across the\nsouthern Baja peninsula and Gulf of California tonight) \ncombined with a frontal boundary approaching nw IL by dawn Thu. \nWe have 20-40% chance of showers and isolated thunderstorms from\nIL river nw on Wed and Wed evening with pops increasing to \n40-60% overnight Wed night nw of the IL river and 20-30% pops as\nfar se as I-55. The front to push se into nw CWA on Thu \nafternoon and through se IL overnight Thu night and see our best\nchances of showers and possible thunderstorms Thu into Friday, \nWPC has slight chance of excessive rainfall Thu and Thu night \nover central IL with marginal risk in southeast IL. Areas se of \nthe IL river have a marginal risk of excessive rainfall on \nFri/Fri night with best qpf shifting into southeast IL. NBM has \na 50-70% chance of over 1.5 inches of rainfall over CWA from Wed\nnight thru Friday night. Highs Thu to range from lower to mid \n70s nw of the IL river to very warm 85-90F in southeast IL. \n\nHigh pressure building into the Great Lakes region Friday night\nand Saturday could shift the rain chances further south of\ncentral IL though NBM still continues chance pops on Fri night \nand Saturday with chance pops se half of CWA Sat night. The \nECMWF, GFS and GEM models show better agreement with high \npressure influencing central IL during this weekend with best \nchances of showers south of the Ohio river. Cooler more fall \nlike temps arrive this this week into early next week with highs\nin the mid 60s to lower 70s Fri thru Monday.",
